.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library defines the provider-neutral
.Dv system.Filesystem
version
.Dv 1.0.0
interface.
Providers expose opaque object handles and resolve names relative to delegated
directory handles.
Backing namespace descriptors remain confined to the provider.
.Pp
The
.Fn filesystemcmp_open
function opens the local component channel that
.Xr serviced 8
injects through
.Ev FILESYSTEMCMP .
The variable contains the decimal descriptor number; the descriptor itself is
the authority.
The
.Xr libservice 3
helper validates and consumes the descriptor, and the library creates one
process-wide correlated client session.
There is no global lookup or fallback path.
Repeated opens return references to that session; callers release each
reference with
.Fn filesystemcmp_close .
.Pp
Names are resolved relative to opaque directory handles; absolute path
resolution is never delegated to the client.
The
.Fn filesystemcmp_open_root
function returns the private scratch root.
The
.Fn filesystemcmp_open_namespace
function opens one of
.Dv FILESYSTEMCMP_NAMESPACE_SCRATCH ,
.Dv FILESYSTEMCMP_NAMESPACE_PERSISTENT ,
or
.Dv FILESYSTEMCMP_NAMESPACE_BUNDLE .
The persistent namespace survives component-worker and consumer restarts.
The persistent object quota counts the namespace root and every regular file
and directory, including closed objects.
The provider reconstructs both byte and object usage at restart.
The bundle namespace is rooted at the consumer's verified
.Pa .cap
bundle and rejects every mutation.
Availability is reported by
.Dv FILESYSTEMCMP_FEATURE_PERSISTENT
and
.Dv FILESYSTEMCMP_FEATURE_BUNDLE ;
an unavailable namespace fails with
.Er ENOENT .
Only
.Xr serviced 8
supplies the provider's persistent and bundle directory descriptors.
There is no consumer-supplied component options string, and consumers never
identify backing paths.
Handles carry generations so stale close, lookup, or I/O requests fail closed.
Typed calls cover feature negotiation, root acquisition, lookup, open,
create, bounded positional I/O, stat, unlink, rename, and handle close.
Client-side validation rejects absolute or multi-component names, dot
components, oversized inline I/O, and unknown flag bits before transport.
.Pp
The path-context API supplies UNIX-like relative path resolution without
adding ambient filesystem authority.
Each context starts at the root of its selected delegated namespace and owns
an independent logical current directory.
Absolute paths begin at that delegated root, not at the host root.
The
.Fn filesystemcmp_path_chdir
operation is transactional: it changes the logical cwd only after the complete
path resolves to a directory.
Dot components and repeated separators are accepted, and
.Ql ..
at the namespace root remains at that root.
The provider process never calls
.Xr chdir 2 ;
all resolution continues to use opaque directory handles.
Changing the logical cwd is client-local and is therefore not an audit event;
the provider continues to audit denied resolution and every namespace
mutation.
Multiple contexts in one process can therefore change directory concurrently
without interfering with one another.
The context retains ancestor handles so relative lookup and parent traversal
remain capability-confined.
.Pp
The logical current directory is distinct from the delegated namespace root.
.Fn filesystemcmp_path_chdir
may select any directory beneath that fixed root, but it never changes the
root or widens the caller's authority.
A program that needs relative references immediately after startup should
create its path context and set its initial logical cwd before consuming those
references.
A future re-root operation, if provided, must create a separate context rooted
at an already-authorized directory handle; it must not mutate an existing
context or accept a host pathname.
The string returned by
.Fn filesystemcmp_path_getcwd
is the context's logical spelling.
It can become stale if another context renames an ancestor; retained handles
still preserve correct relative-resolution authority.
Callers own handles returned by
.Fn filesystemcmp_path_lookup
and
.Fn filesystemcmp_path_create .
The path-context read, write, stat, sync, and close operations use the same
encapsulated client session, so an application using the path API does not
need to acquire or retain a separate low-level client merely to consume a
returned handle.
Path-context objects are process-bound and must not be reused after
.Xr fork 2 .
Provider errors return
.Li -1
and set
.Va errno .
Typed operations are synchronous convenience calls over the asynchronous
.Xr libchannel 3
transport.
The channel reply token correlates concurrent calls; the filesystem protocol
adds only its typed header and payload schema.
A successful reply means the operation completed in the provider and is
visible to later requests on that namespace.
.Fn filesystemcmp_sync
is the explicit persistent-storage durability boundary.
Sync a regular-file handle after writes, and sync every affected directory
handle after create, unlink, or rename.
Scratch sync succeeds as a no-op.
After an ambiguous peer disconnect, mutating operations must not be retried
blindly.
The opaque client is process-bound.
The injected component session is retained as process-lifetime authority;
.Fn filesystemcmp_close
releases a library borrow, and a later
.Fn filesystemcmp_open
reacquires the same managed session without creating another channel reader.
If the provider returns a malformed typed reply, the library marks this
process-lifetime authority terminal.
The failing operation and every later operation return
.Er EPROTO
without sending another request, including after public close and reopen.
This fail-closed state prevents continued use of a provider that has violated
the negotiated protocol.
After
.Xr fork 2 ,
the child cannot reuse the parent's session and must open its own injected
authority if one exists.
Bulk I/O may use
.Xr libshmring 3 .
.Pp
The
.Ql filesystemcmp
DTrace provider exposes component-open, message-send, message-receive, and
message-reject probes.
Provider daemons audit authorization, namespace mutation, and denied
resolution decisions through OpenBSM.
.Sh SEE ALSO
